    faqs: [
        { title: 'What is the difference between RISC and CISC?', answer: 'RISC uses simple, fixed-length instructions (one operation per instruction, load/store memory access). CISC uses complex, variable-length instructions (can do memory operations directly). Modern x86 CPUs translate CISC instructions to RISC-like micro-operations internally.' },
        { title: 'How does a pipeline improve performance?', answer: 'A pipeline overlaps instruction execution like an assembly line. While one instruction is in Execute, another is in Decode, and a third is being Fetched. Ideal speedup equals the number of stages. Hazards (data, control, structural) reduce actual speedup.' },
        { title: 'What is a cache miss?', answer: 'A cache miss occurs when requested data is not in the cache. Compulsory miss: first access. Capacity miss: cache is too small. Conflict miss: mapping causes eviction. Solutions: larger cache, higher associativity, prefetching, and cache-friendly code.' },
        { title: 'How does branch prediction work?', answer: 'The processor predicts whether a branch will be taken and speculatively executes the predicted path. 2-bit saturating counters remember branch history. Tournament predictors combine local and global predictors. Mispredictions flush the pipeline (10-20 cycle penalty).' },
        { title: 'What is Amdahl\'s Law?', answer: 'Amdahl\'s Law: Speedup = 1/((1-P) + P/N), where P is the parallelizable fraction and N is the number of processors. Even with infinite processors, speedup is limited by the sequential portion. 10% sequential code limits speedup to 10x regardless of core count.' },
        { title: 'What is the memory wall?', answer: 'The memory wall is the growing gap between CPU and DRAM speed. CPUs improve ~50% per year while DRAM latency improves ~7% per year. Caches, prefetching, out-of-order execution, and multithreading help hide memory latency but cannot eliminate the fundamental gap.' },
        { title: 'How does out-of-order execution work?', answer: 'The processor issues instructions in order but executes them out of order when operands are ready. Register renaming eliminates false dependencies (WAR, WAW). The reorder buffer commits results in program order for correct behavior. This exploits instruction-level parallelism.' },
        { title: 'What is cache coherence?', answer: 'Cache coherence ensures all processors see a consistent view of shared memory. The MESI protocol tracks cache line states (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid). When one core writes, other copies are invalidated. Snooping monitors bus; directory-based scales to more cores.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between latency and throughput?', answer: 'Latency is the time to complete one operation (e.g., 100ns memory access). Throughput is operations per second (e.g., 10 GB/s bandwidth). Pipelining improves throughput without reducing latency. GPUs optimize throughput; CPUs optimize latency.' },
        { title: 'How does virtual memory translation work?', answer: 'The MMU translates virtual addresses: TLB check first (fast path). On TLB miss, walk the page table (4 levels on x86-64). Each level indexes into a table of page entries. The final entry gives the physical frame number. Page faults trigger OS intervention to load the page.' },
        { title: 'What is SIMD?', answer: 'SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) processes multiple data elements with one instruction. AVX-512 operates on sixteen 32-bit floats simultaneously. Useful for image processing, scientific computing, and ML inference. Compilers can auto-vectorize loops into SIMD instructions.' },
        { title: 'What is speculative execution?', answer: 'Speculative execution runs instructions before knowing if they are needed (after predicted branches). If the prediction is correct, the results are committed. If wrong, the pipeline flushes and rolls back. This hides branch latency but was exploited by Spectre/Meltdown attacks.' },
        { title: 'What is a pipeline hazard?', answer: 'Data hazard: instruction needs a result not yet computed (solved by forwarding/stalling). Control hazard: branch outcome unknown (solved by prediction). Structural hazard: hardware conflict (solved by duplication or stalling). Hazards reduce pipeline efficiency below the ideal.' },
        { title: 'How do GPUs differ from CPUs architecturally?', answer: 'CPUs: few cores (8-64), large caches, complex control (OoO, branch prediction), optimize for single-thread latency. GPUs: thousands of simple cores, small caches, hardware multithreading, optimize for data-parallel throughput. GPUs trade latency for massive parallelism.' },
        { title: 'What is the role of the TLB?', answer: 'The TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) caches virtual-to-physical address translations. Without TLB, every memory access requires a page table walk (4 memory accesses on x86-64). TLB hit rate is typically >99%. TLB misses are expensive (10-100 cycles for the page walk).' },
        { title: 'What is IEEE 754 floating point?', answer: 'IEEE 754 represents real numbers with sign, exponent, and mantissa. Single precision: 32 bits (1+8+23), ~7 decimal digits. Double: 64 bits (1+11+52), ~16 digits. Special values: +-infinity, NaN, denormals. Rounding and limited precision cause representation errors.' },
        { title: 'What causes false sharing?', answer: 'False sharing occurs when different cores modify different variables that share the same cache line. The coherence protocol invalidates the entire line on each write, even though the data is logically independent. Solution: pad data to cache line boundaries or restructure data layout.' },
        { title: 'What is the difference between Harvard and von Neumann architecture?', answer: 'Von Neumann: single memory for instructions and data (shared bus, simpler design). Harvard: separate memories for instructions and data (parallel access, used in DSPs). Modified Harvard: separate L1 caches for instructions and data but unified main memory (modern CPUs).' },
        { title: 'How does prefetching improve performance?', answer: 'Prefetching loads data into the cache before it is needed, hiding memory latency. Hardware prefetchers detect sequential and strided access patterns. Software prefetch instructions give explicit hints. Too aggressive prefetching wastes bandwidth and evicts useful data.' },
        { title: 'What is dark silicon?', answer: 'Dark silicon refers to transistors that cannot be simultaneously powered due to thermal constraints. As transistors shrink, power density increases. Not all cores can run at full frequency. Solutions: heterogeneous computing, accelerators, and dynamically activating subsets of the chip.' },
    ],
